<section class="reference-section" id="markandeya-shiva"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Markandeya and Shiva · The Conquest of Death</h2><a href="/markandeya-shiva.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Source transparency.</strong> This reader presents the widely known Kalasamhara devotional tradition. It is not labelled as a literal translation of one supplied chapter because the owner-supplied Puranic passages preserve related but distinct accounts.</p><ol><li><strong>Skanda Purana, Part XX · Prabhasa Khanda, Prabhasa-kshetra-mahatmya 209.1–44.</strong><small>Printed pp. 543–546; supplied PDF pp. 177–180. Markandeya worships the linga; the chapter then recounts his father Mrikandu, a prediction of the boy’s approaching death, the boy’s reverence, the blessings of the Seven Sages and Brahma, and his life extending across a kalpa. This version does not contain the noose confrontation with Yama.</small></li><li><strong>Shiva Mahapurana · Umāsaṃhitā 4.40.</strong><small>Supplied second-half Gita Press PDF p. 420. Markandeya is named as a great, long-lived devotee who attained his condition through Shiva’s grace. The passage is a concise affirmation, not a full narration.</small></li><li><strong>Skanda Purana, Part I · Maheshvara Khanda, Kedara Khanda 32.18–91.</strong><small>Printed pp. 270–276; supplied PDF pp. 274–280. In the parallel story of King Sveta, Yama’s messengers cannot seize a devotee absorbed in Shiva; Shiva burns Kala, the devotee asks for Kala’s restoration, and cosmic order resumes. The reader uses this only as parallel Shaiva context and does not rename Sveta as Markandeya.</small></li></ol><p>The familiar sequence—Markandeya embraces the linga, Yama’s noose encircles devotee and linga, and Shiva manifests as Kalantaka or Kalasamhara—is identified as devotional tradition. Details of Shiva striking or killing Yama vary in temple, regional and literary tellings. The retelling keeps the confrontation non-graphic and treats Yama as Dharmaraja, not as a demonic antagonist.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated devotional interpretations. The figures, shrine and landscape are imagined; no historical portrait, temple interior or archaeological reconstruction is claimed.</p></div></section>
